hypotheses.statement:
Suppose $T_\omega\ge0$ and $K_\omega=K_\omega^*$ are measurable matrices, $\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}T_\omega=E$ and $K_\omega$ is supported on $T_\omega$ almost everywhere, and $$\int_\Omega\bigl(\lVert T_\omega\rVert_{\mathrm{op}}
                  +\lVert K_\omega\rVert_{\mathrm{op}}\bigr)\,\,\mathrm d\nu(\omega)
 <\infty.$$

Suppose that measurable coefficients $\mu_{ij,k}:\Xi_D\to\mathbb C$ satisfy $$\label{eq:frequency-coefficient-condition}
 |\mu_{ij,k}(\xi)|\leq C_1,
 \qquad
 \sum_{k\in\mathcal I}\int_{\Xi_D}
           |\mu_{ij,k}(\xi)|^2\,\,\mathrm d\gamma(\xi)\leq C_1^2$$ for every output index pair $(i,j)$, with the first inequality holding almost everywhere.

outputs.exact_statement:
There is an absolute constant $C$ such that, for every integer $n\ge1$ and every complex $F,G\in L^3(\mathbb R^2)$, $$\label{eq:count-bound}
 \|\mathcal S_n(F,G)\|_{3/2}
 \le C\sqrt n\log(2+n)\|F\|_3\|G\|_3.$$

For $F,G\in L^3(\mathbb R^2)$, all finite annular integrals defining $B_{\varepsilon,R}(F,G)$ are absolutely convergent outside one common null set. On its complement they depend continuously on $(\varepsilon,R)$ in $0<\varepsilon<R<\infty$. Changing representatives changes this family only on a common null set. Moreover, $$\label{eq:finite-annulus-bound}
 \left\|\int_{\varepsilon<|t|<R}
 |F(x+t,y)G(x,y+t)|\,\frac{\,\mathrm dt}{|t|}\right\|_{3/2}
 \le 2\log(R/\varepsilon)\|F\|_3\|G\|_3.$$

On the cone of matrices positive definite on a fixed support, $\Phi$ is smooth and $$d\Phi_P(K)=\frac32\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}(\sqrt P K),\qquad
 d^2\Phi_P(K,L)=\mathcal B_P(K,L).$$ In an orthonormal eigenbasis for the positive eigenvalues $p_i$ of $P$, $$\label{eq:hessian-metric}
 b_P(K)=\frac32\sum_{i,j}
 \frac{|K_{ij}|^2}{\sqrt{p_i}+\sqrt{p_j}}.$$ In particular, $\mathcal B_P$ is a positive definite symmetric bilinear form on the real space of supported Hermitian directions, and $$|\mathcal B_P(K,L)|\le b_P(K)^{1/2}b_P(L)^{1/2}.$$

For a linear map $R:E\to F$ and a Hermitian map $L:F\to F$, the matrix $R^*LR$ is supported on $R^*R$, and $$\label{eq:insertion-bound}
 b_{R^*R}(R^*LR)
 \le\frac34\lVert L\rVert_{\mathrm{op}}^2\Phi(R^*R).$$

For rectangular complex matrices $R_-,R_+$ of the same size, set $R(z)=(1-z)R_-+zR_+$ and $D=R_+-R_-$. Then $$\label{eq:rank-change-derivative}
 \Phi(R_+^*R_+)-\Phi(R_-^*R_-)
 =3\int_0^1\operatorname{Re}
 \langle R(z)\sqrt{R(z)^*R(z)},D\rangle_{\mathrm{HS}}\,\,\mathrm dz.$$ For every rectangular matrix $R$ and every entry $(i,j)$, $$\label{eq:entry-gradient}
 |(R\sqrt{R^*R})_{ij}|
 \le\lVert\operatorname{row}_iR\rVert_2
       \lVert\operatorname{col}_jR\rVert_2.$$

For Hermitian maps $D_v:H_v\to H_v$, put $D_* =\max_v\lVert D_v\rVert_{\mathrm{op}}$. Then $$\label{eq:mixed-trace}
 \left|\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}(D_0W_0D_1W_1D_2W_2)\right|
 \le\frac53D_*\sum_{v=0}^2b_{T_v}(R_v^*D_vR_v).$$ The constant is independent of the dimensions and no Gram is assumed invertible.

For fixed finite arrays and arbitrary positive rates, $$\label{eq:heat-plane-dissipation}
 2\partial_sJ(d,s)\le\int_{\Pi_d}\sum_w
 \left[-\alpha_w+
 \sqrt2\max\{0,\Sigma_\alpha/2-\alpha_w\}\right]V_w\,\,\mathrm d\pi_d.$$ In particular, put $\lambda=11/10$. For each permutation of $(1,\lambda,\lambda)$, $$\label{eq:heat-strict}
 \begin{gathered}
 -\partial_sJ(d,s)\ge c_*\int_{\Pi_d}\sum_wV_w\,\,\mathrm d\pi_d,\\
 c_* =\tfrac12\min\{1-\tfrac35\sqrt2,
                      \tfrac{11}{10}-\tfrac12\sqrt2\}>0.
 \end{gathered}$$

Fix $v\ne w$, retain the weighted block $C=C_{v,w}$, and delete the other block of the star at $v$. For arbitrary positive rates, set $$\begin{aligned}
 e^e_{v,w}&=\Phi(CC^*),\qquad
 V^e_{v,w}=b_{C^*C}(C^*N_vC),\qquad
 Y^e_{v,w}=b_{CC^*}(CN_wC^*),\\
 J^e_{v,w}(s)&=\iint_{\mathbb R^2}e^e_{v,w}\,\,\mathrm dp_v\,\,\mathrm dp_w.
 \end{aligned}$$ The plane integrals of $e^e_{v,w}$, $V^e_{v,w}$, and $Y^e_{v,w}$ are independent of $d$. Moreover, $$\label{eq:heat-single-edge}
 -2\partial_sJ^e_{v,w}(s)
 =\iint_{\mathbb R^2}
      (\alpha_vV^e_{v,w}+\alpha_wY^e_{v,w})\,\,\mathrm dp_v\,\,\mathrm dp_w.$$ If $n_e$ denotes the discrete $L^3$ norm of this edge’s unweighted array, then, whenever $\alpha_vs_0,\alpha_ws_0\ge h^2$ and $s_1>s_0$, $$\label{eq:heat-single-edge-integral}
 \int_{s_0}^{s_1}\iint_{\mathbb R^2}
      (\alpha_vV^e_{v,w}+\alpha_wY^e_{v,w})
          \,\,\mathrm dp_v\,\,\mathrm dp_w\,\,\mathrm ds
 \le2M n_e^3.$$ For the original two-block star, $Y_{v,w}\le Y^e_{v,w}$.

Use the masked arrays above and variances $\sigma_v=\alpha_vs$, where $\boldsymbol\alpha$ is any permutation of $(1,11/10,11/10)$. With $n_v$ denoting the norms of the original arrays, $$\label{eq:repeated-dissipation}
 \int_{s_-}^{s_+}\int_{\Pi_0}\sum_vV_v\,\mathrm d\pi_0\,\mathrm ds
 \le C\left(\sum_vn_v^3+n\bigl(n_0^3+n_0^2(n_1+n_2)\bigr)\right).$$

Let $\mathcal I\subset\mathbb Z$ be finite and nonempty, and set $L_k=2^k$. Let $A_0,A_1,A_2$ be finite arrays on the lattice $u_i=ih$, with the norms $n_v$ defined in the heat-flow preliminaries. Suppose that measurable coefficients $\mu_{ij,k}:\Xi_D\to\mathbb C$ satisfy $$\label{eq:frequency-coefficient-condition}
 |\mu_{ij,k}(\xi)|\leq C_1,
 \qquad
 \sum_{k\in\mathcal I}\int_{\Xi_D}
           |\mu_{ij,k}(\xi)|^2\,\,\mathrm d\gamma(\xi)\leq C_1^2$$ for every output index pair $(i,j)$, with the first inequality holding almost everywhere. If $$\label{eq:frequency-mesh}
 h^2\leq\frac{q\min_{k\in\mathcal I}L_k^2}{16D^2},$$ then $$\label{eq:frequency-finite-estimate}
 \left|h^3\int_{\Xi_D}\sum_{k\in\mathcal I}\sum_{i,j,l}
 A_0(i,j)\mu_{ij,k}(\xi)A_1(j,l)A_2(l,i)
 \mathop{\mathrm{Dil}}_{L_k}H_\xi(u_i+u_j+u_l)\,\,\mathrm d\gamma(\xi)\right|
 \leq C(q,C_1)\prod_{v=0}^2n_v.$$

Fix distinct vertices $v,w\in\{0,1\}$, so that $\{v,w\}=\{0,1\}$. Let the edge joining $v,w$ have array $B$, indexed in the original endpoint order $(0,1)$, and let the edge joining $v,2$ have array $E$, indexed in the order $(v,2)$. Use variances $(\sigma_v,\sigma_w,\sigma_2)=(\theta s,s,s)$, where $0<\theta\leq1/16$ and $h^2\leq\theta s$. Write $\,\mathrm d\nu_s(d)=g_{(1-\theta)s}(d)\,\,\mathrm dd$. There is a nonnegative array $\mathcal H_E(i,j)$, independent of $s,\theta$, such that $$\begin{aligned}
 &\int_{\mathbb R}\int_{\Pi_d}
       \|C_{v,w}\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2\|C_{v,2}\|_{\mathrm{HS}}
                  \,\,\mathrm d\pi_d\,\,\mathrm d\nu_s(d)
 \leq C\sum_{i,j}h^2|B(i,j)|^2\mathcal H_E(i,j),
 \label{eq:frequency-boundary-mixed}\\
 &\left(\sum_{i,j}h^2\mathcal H_E(i,j)^3\right)^{1/3}
 \leq C\left(\sum_{m,l}h^2|E(m,l)|^3\right)^{1/3}.
 \label{eq:frequency-boundary-norm}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $(i,j)$ are always in the original order of the endpoints of edge $0$; $i_v,i_w$ denote the same two indices in the order $(v,w)$. The input arrays $B,E$ are extended by zero to $\mathbb Z^2$.

Let $q,D,\Xi_D,\mathcal I$ be as in Proposition <a href="#prop:frequency-block" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:frequency-block">17</a>. Suppose that $\mu_{a,b,k}(\xi)$ is constant in $(a,b)$ on every cell of one finite rectangular partition, independent of $\xi$ and common to all $k$. Suppose its values on these cells are bounded measurable functions of $\xi$, and that $$\label{eq:frequency-continuous-condition}
 |\mu_{a,b,k}(\xi)|\leq C_1,
 \qquad
 \sum_k\int_{\Xi_D}|\mu_{a,b,k}(\xi)|^2\,\,\mathrm d\gamma(\xi)
 \leq C_1^2.$$ Define $$\label{eq:frequency-continuous-kernel}
 \mathcal K_{a,b}(t)
   =\int_{\Xi_D}\sum_{k\in\mathcal I}
          \mu_{a,b,k}(\xi)\mathop{\mathrm{Dil}}_{L_k}H_\xi(t)\,\,\mathrm d\gamma(\xi).$$ Then, for complex $G_v\in L^3(\mathbb R^2)$, $$\label{eq:frequency-continuous-estimate}
 |\Lambda_{\mathcal K}(G_0,G_1,G_2)|
       \leq C(q,C_1)\prod_{v=0}^2\|G_v\|_3.$$

For complex $F,G\in L^3(\mathbb R^2)$, define $$B_*(F,G)(x,y)
 =\sup_{0<\varepsilon<R<\infty}|B_{\varepsilon,R}(F,G)(x,y)|$$ on the common full-measure set of Lemma <a href="#lem:finite-truncations" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:finite-truncations">3</a>, and set it to zero elsewhere. This function is measurable, and there is an absolute constant $C_M$ such that $$\label{eq:hard-maximal-bound}
 \|B_*(F,G)\|_p\le C_M\|F\|_3\|G\|_3.$$ The supremum includes both finite hard truncation endpoints. Moreover, $B_*$ is a continuous map from $L^3(\mathbb R^2)\times L^3(\mathbb R^2)$ to $L^p(\mathbb R^2)$ and is the unique continuous extension of its values on compactly supported smooth pairs.

For every complex $F,G\in L^3(\mathbb R^2)$, the joint limit $$B(F,G)=\lim_{\substack{\varepsilon\downarrow0\\R\uparrow\infty}}
 B_{\varepsilon,R}(F,G)$$ exists almost everywhere and in $L^p(\mathbb R^2)$, and $$\|B(F,G)\|_p\le C_M\|F\|_3\|G\|_3.$$ More precisely, the entire tail converges in the maximal sense $$\label{eq:maximal-tail-convergence}
 \left\|\sup_{\substack{0<\varepsilon<1/n\\R>n}}
 |B_{\varepsilon,R}(F,G)-B(F,G)|\right\|_p
 \longrightarrow0.$$ The operator $B$ is the unique bounded complex bilinear extension of the principal-value operator on compactly supported smooth inputs.

For every complex $L^3$ triple, each finite flat truncation is absolutely integrable, and $$\sup_{0<\varepsilon<R<\infty}
 |\mathcal L_{\varepsilon,R}(F_0,F_1,F_2)|
 \le C_M\prod_{v=0}^2\|F_v\|_3.$$ Its joint scalar principal value exists and equals $\int_{\mathbb R^2}F_0 B(F_1,F_2)$. This limiting form is the unique bounded complex trilinear extension of its compactly supported smooth definition.

For every complex $G_0,G_1,G_2\in L^3(\mathbb R^2)$, each finite simplex truncation is absolutely integrable, and $$\sup_{0<\varepsilon<R<\infty}
 |\Lambda_{\varepsilon,R}(G_0,G_1,G_2)|
 \le C_M\prod_{v=0}^2\|G_v\|_3.$$ Its joint scalar principal value exists and is the unique bounded complex trilinear extension of its compactly supported smooth definition. The optimal constants in the uniform finite flat and simplex scalar estimates are equal.
